\begin{aligned}
&31 = 3 \cdot 10 + 1 \\
&3 = 3 \cdot 1 + 0
\end{aligned}
\] From the first equation, we get \( 1 = 31 - 3 \cdot 10 \). Thus, the modular inverse of 3 modulo 31 is \(-10\). Since we need a positive integer between 0 and 30, we add 31 to \(-10\) to get \( 21 \). So, \( n = 21 \). Next, we need to find \( (2^n)^3 - 2 \pmod{31} \). This simplifies to \( 2^{3 \cdot 21} - 2 \pmod{31} \), which is \( 2^{63} - 2 \pmod{31} \). We can use Fermat's Little Theorem, which states that \( a^{p-1} \equiv 1 \pmod{p} \) for any prime \( p \) and integer \( a \) not divisible by \( p \). Here, \( 2^{30} \equiv 1 \pmod{31} \), so \( 2^{63} \equiv 2^{30 \cdot 2 + 3} \equiv (2^{30})^2 \cdot 2^3 \equiv 1^2 \cdot 2^3 \equiv 8 \pmod{31} \). Therefore, \( 2^{63} - 2 \equiv 8 - 2 \equiv 6 \pmod{31} \). The answer is \( 6 \). </think> <answer>6</answer>

